{"word": "wealthy", "source": "freedictionaryapi", "data": {"word": "wealthy", "entries": [{"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "adjective", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈwɛl.θi/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈwɛld̪(ʱ)i/", "tags": []}], "forms": [{"word": "wealthier", "tags": ["comparative"]}, {"word": "more wealthy", "tags": ["comparative"]}, {"word": "wealthiest", "tags": ["superlative"]}, {"word": "most wealthy", "tags": ["superlative"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "Possessing financial wealth; rich.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "As a result of these causes the grazier marks himself off fairly sharply from the rest of Australia. He has always spent a considerable proportion of the time in the capital cities, in each of which he has formed a club to which, besides the graziers, only a few of the wealthiest and most prominent of the city dwellers are admitted.", "reference": "1963, Colin Clark, Australian Hopes and Fears, page 75:"}, {"text": "As people stall feed through the years, or if they are wealthier to begin with, the money is used to build houses and buy metal roof sheets, or to buy bicycles, ox-carts and breeding cows.", "reference": "1984, Cornelia Butler Flora, Animals in the Farming System:"}, {"text": "Gleefully and legally intruding upon the sanctum of the wealthy linksters, we walked the mitigation meadows.", "reference": "2010, Robert Michael Pyle, chapter 18, in Mariposa Road, Boston: Houghton Mifflin Harcourt, page 158:"}, {"text": "In order to grant the rich these pleasures, the social contract is reconfigured. […] The public realm is privatised, the regulations restraining the ultra–wealthy and the companies they control are abandoned, and Edwardian levels of inequality are almost fetishised.", "reference": "2013 May 17, George Monbiot, “Money just makes the rich suffer”, in The Guardian Weekly, volume 188, number 23, page 19:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "transla